<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Cerdanya</h1><p class="st-meta">798 CE – 1117 CE (approx.) · capital Hix</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Pyrenean county of the Catalan heartland whose counts founded Sant Martí del Canigó before it passed to Barcelona in 1117.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The high valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> came under Frankish rule around 798 and was organised as a county of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marca-hispanica">Marca Hispanica</a>, often held with neighbouring Urgell. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunifred-of-urgell">Sunifred</a>, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wilfred the Hairy</a>, held it in the 830s and 840s, and Wilfred himself became count in 870.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From 897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> had its own line of counts descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wilfred</a>, controlling the valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conflent">Conflent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berga">Berga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monastery-of-santa-maria-de-ripoll">Ripoll</a>. The counts were great patrons of the church: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wifred-ii-of-cerdanya">Wifred II</a> founded the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sant-marti-del-canigo">Sant Martí del Canigó</a> around 1009, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliba">Oliba</a> became abbot of Ripoll and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Vic. The county lived from mountain herding and Pyrenean trade routes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernat-guillem-of-cerdanya">Bernat Guillem</a> died without heirs in 1117, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iii-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona</a>. It became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon</a>, and in 1659 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> split the valley between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-cerdanya.jpg" alt="County of Cerdanya" width="320" height="344"><figcaption>Image: Nekto; derivative work by Lliura · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comtats_catalans_s._VIII-XII.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>798 CE – 1117 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hix</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sunifred, Wifred II of Cerdanya, Bernat Guillem</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cerdagne, Comtat de Cerdanya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=958&amp;focus=county-of-cerdanya" aria-label="County of Cerdanya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4727 669 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Cerdanya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5047 925L5139 672M5047 925L5053 958"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5139 672h0M5053 958h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5047 925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5047 925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=958&amp;focus=county-of-cerdanya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
